                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the brand–supply-chain gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Nordmilk's ultimatum puts <strong>€242M (22% of revenue)</strong> and the group's richest ~18% nutritional-ingredient margin at risk. Around <strong>960 farms (40%)</strong> still cannot evidence their carbon and welfare numbers. Before you spend a euro, separate what Cláirseach can <em>credibly claim today</em> from what the brand will need to promise. The trap is over-claiming — a bold pledge the audited reality cannot back becomes a <em>greenwashing</em> exposure the moment Nordmilk's auditor pulls a sample. State the one claim you can defend, and locate the revenue and margin you must protect first.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Commit the supply chain (this gates everything)`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `This is the McLoughlin core: the brand can never promise more than the supply chain can verify, so the supply commitment comes <strong>first</strong> and <strong>bounds</strong> the brand. Choose how far and how fast to verify — <strong>sequence the farms supplying Nordmilk volume first</strong>, or scatter effort across all 2,400 with no priority. Set the co-op / farmer / shared funding split (it plays at the November AGM on farmer milk price), and commit budget to closing the ~€3.4M gap. Verification is what gates every premium that follows.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Build the national brand programme`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Now design the brand the €9M funds. Choose the <strong>architecture</strong> — a B2B ingredient brand for buyers like Nordmilk is where 68% of your revenue lives; a consumer-only campaign ignores the buyer holding the €242M. Choose <strong>positioning</strong> that rivals can't copy: New Zealand and the Dutch co-op also say “sustainable, natural, trusted” — your edge is <em>what you can prove farm by farm</em> in Origin Green. And fund the <strong>proof system</strong> that turns a farm's carbon number into a claim Nordmilk's auditor accepts. Keep every promise inside Round 2's verified reality.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Allocate the budget & defend under uncertainty`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Allocate what's left across <strong>B2B trade marketing</strong> and a <strong>consumer pull</strong> campaign, then choose the scenario you build to survive. The premium (3–8%) and demand lift (2–6%) are wide, uncertain ranges — a plan that only works at the <em>top</em> of both is a hope, not a plan. Stress-test the floor. Then defend to the co-op board and the Nordmilk buyer that your brand spend never outran your verified supply. If buyer confidence falls too far, Nordmilk re-tenders to a NZ or Dutch rival and the €242M walks.`,

