                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_claim_right:   `<strong>That's the disciplined claim.</strong> Scoping the promise to the 60% of farms you can already evidence keeps the brand inside the proven reality. When Nordmilk's auditor pulls a sample, the claim survives — that's the difference between a green brand and a liability.`,
                    coach_r1_overclaim:     `<strong>“Verified low-carbon” across the whole co-op?</strong> Forty percent of your farms can't prove their carbon numbers yet. Promise it now and prove it later, and you've built the claim ahead of the supply chain — McLoughlin's exact failure mode. The brand cannot promise more than the farms can verify.`,
                    coach_r1_netzero:       `<strong>A net-zero-by-2030 pledge is a headline, not a claim you can defend.</strong> With 960 farms unverified, this is greenwashing waiting for an NGO report. The bolder the unbacked promise, the bigger the exposure when an auditor tests it.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You scoped the claim to what's verified AND aimed at the €242M Nordmilk volume and the ~18% margin behind it. That's where the value — and the risk — actually sits. Now you can build supply-then-brand in the right order.`,
                    coach_r1_commodity:     `<strong>Commodity butter is the litres, not the value.</strong> It earns ~7% and Nordmilk isn't auditing it. The €242M and the rich nutritional margin are what's at risk — protect that volume first, not the bulk tonnage.`,
                    coach_r1_consumer:      `<strong>The domestic shopper isn't holding the €242M.</strong> Sixty-eight percent of your revenue is exported as ingredients to buyers like Nordmilk. Building brand love at home first ignores where the concentration risk actually is.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how greenwashing starts.</strong> Low discipline about what the farms can verify means the brand drifts ahead of the proof. Name honestly what you can and cannot yet claim, or the audit will name it for you.`,
                    coach_r2_sequenced:     `<strong>That's the right sequencing.</strong> Verifying the farms that supply Nordmilk volume first protects the €242M with 24 months on the clock, then spreads to the rest. You've put the verification where the at-risk value is — exactly what a co-op with a deadline must do.`,
                    coach_r2_unsequenced:   `<strong>100% with no priority order spends effort in the wrong places.</strong> You're certifying small farms that don't supply Nordmilk volume before the ones that do. Admirable ambition, but with 24 months the at-risk volume must come first. Which farms qualify the €242M?`,
                    coach_r2_minimum:       `<strong>Scraping past the audit is a one-customer fix.</strong> It might satisfy Nordmilk this cycle, but the further ~15% of nutritional revenue is watching the signal — and a thin verification base can't support a national brand.`,
                    coach_r2_defer:         `<strong>Brand first, verify later is the cardinal error.</strong> You'd build the claim ahead of the supply chain — and when the audit comes, the brand becomes Exhibit A in a greenwashing story. Which comes first, the claim or the proof?`,
                    coach_r2_shared:        `<strong>A shared model balances the politics and the budget.</strong> A co-op advisory grant plus farmer co-payment closes the gap faster without draining the scarce €9M, and it gives farmers skin in the game ahead of the AGM milk-price test.`,
                    coach_r2_farmer:        `<strong>Loading the cost onto farmers risks an AGM revolt.</strong> Tell 2,400 farmer-owners to fund their own audits and the November AGM judges that against their milk price. Some won't, the gap stays open, and your brand loses its base.`,
                    coach_r2_coop:          `<strong>Co-op-funded protects farmer price but burns the budget.</strong> It plays well at the AGM, yet verification alone could swallow much of the €9M you also need for proof and brand. A shared model stretches the same euro further.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>You've under-funded the gap.</strong> Closing the 40% gap costs ~€3.4M; starve it and verified supply stalls — and every premium downstream is gated by exactly this number. This is the load-bearing spend.`,
                    coach_r3_both:          `<strong>An ingredient brand plus a verified endorsement is the right architecture.</strong> It reduces Nordmilk's risk and gives them a claim to pass to their consumers — the ingredient-branding logic — while a disciplined consumer endorsement adds pull without overrunning the proof.`,
                    coach_r3_b2b:           `<strong>A B2B ingredient brand goes where the money is.</strong> Sixty-eight percent of revenue is exported ingredients, so reducing the buyer's risk is sound. Consider whether a verified endorsement they can pass to their consumers would add pull without overclaiming.`,
                    coach_r3_consumer:      `<strong>A consumer-only campaign ignores the buyer holding the €242M.</strong> A beautiful endorsement aimed at the shopper is a poster when 68% of your revenue depends on Nordmilk and its peers. An ingredient brand that ignores the buyer misses the point.`,
                    coach_r3_corporate:     `<strong>A corporate-reputation campaign doesn't sell the ingredient.</strong> Nordmilk's buyer needs a claim that reduces their risk and supports their consumer story, not a film about Cláirseach the company. Aim the brand at the buyer's problem.`,
                    coach_r3_differentiated:`<strong>That's evidence-based differentiation.</strong> Origin Green farm-by-farm verified data is precisely what New Zealand and the Dutch co-op cannot put on the table. Built into an ingredient brand, that proof — not the adjectives — is your defensible positioning.`,
                    coach_r3_generic:       `<strong>“Sustainable, natural, trusted” is everyone's deck.</strong> Nordmilk's buyer has three identical ones on the desk. What can Cláirseach prove farm by farm in Origin Green that the others can't? That's your brand — not the adjectives.`,
                    coach_r3_price:         `<strong>Competing on price abandons the premium you're trying to earn.</strong> The whole point of verified sustainability is to defend the ~18% margin, not to win a landed-cost race against scaled rivals. Differentiate on proof, not price.`,
                    coach_r3_heritage:      `<strong>Heritage is a nice story but not an auditable claim.</strong> The 1974 co-op narrative won't survive an auditor's sample. Emotion without farm-level evidence is exactly the claim that outruns the supply chain.`,
                    coach_r3_noproof:       `<strong>You've starved the proof system.</strong> Without the infrastructure that turns a farm's carbon audit into a claim Nordmilk's auditor accepts, you have verified farms and a brand but no bridge between them. Proof is the load-bearing investment.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>The brand is drifting ahead of the proof.</strong> Low claim restraint means the campaign promises more than the verified farms can back — that gap is your greenwashing exposure. Pull the claim back inside what Round 2 actually verified.`,
                    coach_r4_notrade:       `<strong>Almost nothing for the B2B buyer?</strong> Nordmilk holds the €242M and needs to be sold the risk-reduction story directly. Trade marketing to the buyer is not optional when 68% of revenue is exported ingredients.`,
                    coach_r4_overpull:      `<strong>A heavy consumer pull while supply is still thin is brand outrunning proof.</strong> Big shopper spend on a claim the farms can't fully back is the greenwashing trap again. Right-size the pull to the verified reality.`,
                    coach_r4_stress:        `<strong>Stress-testing the floor is real planning.</strong> If the plan creates value even at a 3% premium and a 2% demand lift, it's resilient to the uncertainty — not a bet on the optimistic top. That's the discipline the board wants to see.`,
                    coach_r4_optimistic:    `<strong>You've modelled only the top of both ranges.</strong> An 8% premium and a 6% lift, and the plan only works at those numbers? That's not a plan, it's a hope. What's your ROI at 3% and 2% — and what do you protect first when you cut?`,
                    coach_r4_defended:      `<strong>A board-and-buyer-ready defence.</strong> You showed the brand spend never outran verified supply, stress-tested the downside, and kept Nordmilk's confidence — that's a verified promise that holds, not a number that collapses under an audit.`,

